<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Handsome Lake</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1735">1735</a>–<a href="/years/1815">1815</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Sganyodaiyo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Handsome Lake was a Seneca prophet and chief, half-brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornplanter">Cornplanter</a>. After a series of visions in 1799, he preached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/longhouse-religion">Gaiwiio</a> ('Good Message'), which called for temperance, a return to traditional ceremonies and adaptation to farming and family life. His teaching became the Longhouse religion, still practised by many Haudenosaunee people today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/handsome-lake.jpg" alt="Handsome Lake" width="320" height="513" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jesse Cornplanter · No restrictions · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Handsome_Lake_cropped_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
